<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shays' Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1787">1787</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shays' Rebellion was an armed uprising in western Massachusetts in 1786–1787 by indebted farmers, led by the war veteran Daniel Shays, against high taxes and the seizure of property for debt. Its suppression by a state-funded militia exposed the weakness of the national government under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a> and strengthened support for the Constitutional Convention of 1787.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shays-rebellion.jpg" alt="Shays' Rebellion" width="320" height="258" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. Kendrick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shays_forces_flee_Continental_troops,_Springfield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The hill towns of central and western Massachusetts lived largely by subsistence farming and credit, while the coastal merchants who dominated the state government traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>. In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> merchant houses withdrew loans and demanded hard currency, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> merchants passed the demand on to their customers. Farmers who could not pay began to lose land and possessions through the courts. After James Bowdoin became governor in 1785 he stepped up the collection of back taxes, and the legislature added a property tax that even <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a> thought heavier than the people could bear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When the legislature adjourned in August 1786 without considering the petitions sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, protesters calling themselves Regulators stopped the county court from sitting at Northampton on 29 August, then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>, Great Barrington, Concord and Taunton. The state answered with a Riot Act and a suspension of habeas corpus, and on 30 November Job Shattuck was arrested at Groton. With the federal government unable to pay for troops, Benjamin Lincoln raised more than £6,000 from over 125 merchants for an army of 3,000 men from the eastern counties.</p><p>On 25 January 1787 the forces of Shays and Eli Parsons approached the Springfield Armory, unaware that Luke Day had postponed his attack and that his message had been intercepted. William Shepard's militia fired warning shots and then grapeshot from two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, killing four and wounding 20. Lincoln's night march through a snowstorm surprised the rebel camp at Petersham, and at the end of February about 120 rebels who had crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> raided Stockbridge, leading to the bloodiest clash of the rising at Sheffield.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Four thousand people signed confessions in exchange for amnesty. Eighteen men were sentenced to death, but most were pardoned or had their sentences commuted; John Bly and Charles Rose were hanged on 6 December 1787. Shays was pardoned in 1788 and died poor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1825. Bowdoin lost the election of 1787 to John Hancock, and the legislature cut taxes and placed a moratorium on debts. Historians remain divided on how far the rising shaped the Constitution, though most agree it drew some Anti-Federalists towards a stronger government; Massachusetts ratified by 187 votes to 168 on 6 February 1788.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shays's Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Treaty of Paris of 1783 recognised independence, with a western border on the Mississippi, debts and unrest such as <b>Shays' Rebellion</b> of 1786–1787 persuaded leaders to call the Constitutional Convention at Philadelphia in 1787.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
